{"name":"Water Cooler","hex":"#75a7ad","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"water cooler","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dispenser of cooled drinking water.","A location in the workplace where employees gather to chat or gossip."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of the nature of things discussed around a water cooler."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/water_cooler"}],"entries":[{"title":"Water dispenser","blocks":[{"text":"A water dispenser,\nsometimes referred to as a water cooler, is a machine that dispenses and often also cools or heats up water with a refrigeration unit. It is commonly located near the restroom due to closer access to plumbing. A drain line is also provided from the water cooler into the sewer system."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Clickon_Water_Dispenser.jpg/330px-Clickon_Water_Dispenser.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_dispenser","wikidata":"Q1809623"},{"title":"Water","blocks":[{"text":"Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans. Water is also the fluid of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/H2O_2D_labelled.svg/330px-H2O_2D_labelled.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water","wikidata":"Q283"},{"title":"Cooler","blocks":[{"text":"A cooler, portable ice chest, ice box, cool box, chilly bin, or esky (Australia) is an insulated box used to keep food or drink cool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/PortableIceChestPatent1955.jpg/330px-PortableIceChestPatent1955.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooler","wikidata":"Q1976332"},{"title":"Color of water","blocks":[{"text":"The color of water varies with the ambient conditions in which that water is present. While relatively small quantities of water appear to be colorless and transparent, pure water has a slight blue color that becomes deeper as the thickness of the observed sample increases. The hue of water is an intrinsic property and is caused by selective absorption and scattering of blue light. Dissolved elements or suspended impurities may give water a different color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG/330px-Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_of_water","wikidata":"Q5148636"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Two_Types_Of_Sunroc_Water_Coolers.JPG/500px-Two_Types_Of_Sunroc_Water_Coolers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Two_Types_Of_Sunroc_Water_Coolers.JPG/1280px-Two_Types_Of_Sunroc_Water_Coolers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Two_Types_Of_Sunroc_Water_Coolers.JPG/500px-Two_Types_Of_Sunroc_Water_Coolers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn